    words_note='Before the ideas, five words, so that nothing later sounds like jargon. Parameters are the weights and biases you met in Session 1, the numbers training adjusts. A bit is a 0 or a 1, eight bits make a byte, and precision is how many bits each parameter is stored in. Quantisation stores each parameter with fewer bits by rounding it. A file format is the container that packs the parameters. And open weights means the parameters can be downloaded, under a licence. Keep file format and quantisation apart in your head, because mixing them up is the commonest mistake. We start with the first word.',
    word_icons=['bars', 'calculator', 'gear', 'folder', 'key'],
    cover_note='Welcome to Session 7. Last time you saw how a foundation model is made, pretraining and post-training, and the families of models built that way, several of which you can download. That raises our question for today: what does it mean to own, host or rent a model? A model you can download is a very large file of numbers, and its size, its licence and its file format decide the answer. I will show real file sizes that I measured, and you will leave with a decision sheet.',
    outcome_note='That question breaks into three things you will be able to do. You can tell open source from open weights and read a licence for the limits that matter. You can work out by hand how big a model file is, and how quantisation makes it smaller. And you can choose rent, host or own for one of your own tasks, with a break-even sum, and say why. Nothing here needs coding. Multiplication, division and rounding are the hardest steps.',
